# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in real values. Never commit `.env.local`.
#
# Every credential here is server-side only. Do not prefix any of these with
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ — that would inline the secret into the client bundle.

# OpenAI API key, used server-side to write the plain-language explanation of an
# already-computed analysis. The model never produces a figure: it is handed
# verified numbers and returns prose, checked against a schema on the way back.
# https://platform.openai.com/api-keys
#
# With this absent the pool page still works — every figure is computed without
# it — and reports that no explanation is available.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Optional. Which model writes the explanation. Leave blank for the default
# (gpt-5.6-terra, roughly a cent an analysis). The application accepts only
# models it knows the price of, and falls back to the default for anything else
# — the page names whichever model actually wrote the text, so a fallback is
# visible rather than silent.
#
# Known: gpt-5.6-luna, gpt-5.6-terra, gpt-5.6-sol, gpt-6-astra,
#        gpt-5-mini, gpt-4o-mini
#
# Leave it blank to use the default, which is the model this project's own key
# has been measured to reach.
#
# If the page reports that the key is not permitted to use the selected model,
# the cause is usually not billing and not the model's generation: an OpenAI
# project can be scoped to an explicit list of models, and a name outside that
# list answers 403 whether it is new or old. Check the project's model
# permissions in the OpenAI dashboard before trying another name — on this
# project every name above except gpt-5.6-luna is refused, gpt-4o-mini included.
OPENAI_MODEL=

# The Graph API key, used server-side to query the Uniswap v3/v4 subgraphs.
# Sent only as an Authorization header, never in a URL or request body.
# https://thegraph.com/studio/apikeys/
THE_GRAPH_API_KEY=

# Stable Subgraph ID of the Uniswap v3 Ethereum mainnet subgraph, as shown in The
# Graph Explorer. This is NOT a deployment/IPFS id: the gateway's
# /api/subgraphs/id/ endpoint resolves a Subgraph ID to whichever deployment
# indexers report as sufficiently synced, so queries follow published upgrades
# instead of pinning to one frozen version.
# Server-side only: it selects which subgraph the server queries and is never
# read by the browser.
UNISWAP_V3_ETHEREUM_SUBGRAPH_ID=

# Stable Subgraph ID of a Uniswap v4 Ethereum mainnet subgraph, same rules as
# above. Not a credential — a Subgraph ID is public — so the working one is
# written out rather than left blank.
#
# It was chosen by measurement, not by popularity, and the popular ones are why
# that mattered. Of six published v4 Ethereum subgraphs, the most queried (9M a
# month) answers `_meta` but fails every real query: its indexers report "too far
# behind" or "no attestation: indexing_error". Two more are healthy but model a
# different schema — their `Pool` has no `feeTier` and no `token0`. Two have no
# allocations at all. The one below answers, indexes within seconds of head, and
# carries the fields a v4 read needs: feeTier, tickSpacing, hooks, and both
# currencies including the zero address for native ether.
UNISWAP_V4_ETHEREUM_SUBGRAPH_ID=EzLH76FWsZUSBTfp2P6CV7cqN56e1rSt2uWtqtsUMEeb

# Uniswap v3 on Base and on Arbitrum One, same rules again, and public like
# the v4 one. Chosen by measurement on 2026-09-25, asking each candidate the
# week's busiest thousand pool-days with their pool cards — the heaviest query
# the pool pages send. Of four Base subgraphs one answered (the others errored);
# of four Arbitrum ones two did, and this is the one from Base's publisher.
UNISWAP_V3_BASE_SUBGRAPH_ID=43Hwfi3dJSoGpyas9VwNoDAv55yjgGrPpNSmbQZArzMG
UNISWAP_V3_ARBITRUM_SUBGRAPH_ID=FbCGRftH4a3yZugY7TnbYgPJVEv2LvMT6oF1fxPe9aJM

# Uniswap v4 on Arbitrum One and on Base, public like the others. Arbitrum's,
# measured on 2026-09-26: the week's busiest thousand pool-days in 0.7 s, one
# block behind the chain, with the PoolManager Uniswap publishes for Arbitrum.
# Base's, on 2026-09-27: the same in 3-4 s, seconds behind, with Base's
# PoolManager. The other public Base v4 subgraph (HNCFA9…) answered only
# indexing errors, and this one had failed the day before, so the health
# check asks every subgraph hourly.
UNISWAP_V4_ARBITRUM_SUBGRAPH_ID=D1VHPU6cXXSC8eaApWCjCnPcTZQFSYCpGoDAvt4ogDWh
UNISWAP_V4_BASE_SUBGRAPH_ID=Gqm2b5J85n1bhCyDMpGbtbVn4935EvvdyHdHrx3dibyj

# Uniswap v4 on Unichain, public like the others, with Unichain's PoolManager
# (0x1f98…0004). Replaced on 2026-10-04: the one chosen on 2026-09-27
# (EoCvJ5…) had stopped on an indexing error, and every indexer refused its
# data. This one, the most staked of the public candidates, answered the week's
# day table in about three seconds, a pair in one and a half, with no lag behind
# the chain. Unichain has no v3 id, so src/lib/chains/chains.ts reads it for v4
# alone.
UNISWAP_V4_UNICHAIN_SUBGRAPH_ID=GjUSgVwFYnUAXf9ixkJFzrwkG1CdXhfdBKswLJEtZ5e

# Uniswap v3 and v4 on OP Mainnet and on Polygon, public like the others,
# measured 2026-09-27 with the week's day table. OP Mainnet's answered in about
# a second each, seconds behind, with the factory and PoolManager Uniswap
# publishes there; of the other candidates, most model a different schema and
# one has no indexer. Polygon's v4 answered in under two seconds, and prices
# its tokens in POL, the chain's own currency. Both public Polygon v3 subgraphs
# answer the day table and fail every query that filters or orders pools
# ("bad indexers"), so Polygon's v3 search and pair lists are built from the
# day table, as Base's are.
UNISWAP_V3_OPTIMISM_SUBGRAPH_ID=49LkWjoVKd3bM9ZrMdFgYkjaCuVj4ExZttQi6XfbcPpG
UNISWAP_V4_OPTIMISM_SUBGRAPH_ID=Ab7CAcb1yPXUU2j9Bha1BPDHjE5QqUtjC3rPYyAyodJA
UNISWAP_V3_POLYGON_SUBGRAPH_ID=3hCPRGf4z88VC5rsBKU5AA9FBBq5nF3jbKJG7VZCbhjm
UNISWAP_V4_POLYGON_SUBGRAPH_ID=2CB2uQxcDKWDenagn2z17KQVCtfwSx5eXYuvqTciRTJu

# Uniswap v3 and v4 on BNB Chain and on Avalanche, and v3 on Celo, public like
# the others. Measured 2026-10-04: each answered every query the pages send —
# the week's day table, a pool's card, state and month of prices, a pair's fee
# tiers, both searches, a v4 pair, the hook counts, the native price — seconds
# behind the chain and with no indexing errors, and each prices its tokens in
# the chain's own currency (BNB about $788, AVAX about $11, CELO about $0.10).
#   - BNB Chain's v3 (factory 0xdb1d…61f7) gave the day table in 1.6-3.2 s and
#     a search in 1-6 s; its v4 (PoolManager 0x28e2…e9df) the day table in
#     7-7.6 s over 610 pools, and a pair in 0.5-4.2 s.
#   - Avalanche's v3 (factory 0x740b…1bad) the day table in 6.3-7.6 s and a
#     search in 2-6 s; its v4 (PoolManager 0x0638…bc85) the day table in
#     2.5-4 s and a pair in 0.6-3.1 s.
#   - Celo's v3 the day table in 8.5-9.8 s and a search in 1-1.6 s. Its
#     factory entity says mainnet's 0x1f98…f984, which is written into the
#     subgraph; the pools it lists are Celo's own factory's, 0xafe2…acdec,
#     checked by CREATE2 (src/lib/uniswap/v3PositionManager.ts says how).
# None of the three v3 subgraphs keeps positions, so there is no smart-money
# page for these chains; and Celo has no v4 id, so src/lib/chains/chains.ts
# reads it for v3 alone.
UNISWAP_V3_BNB_SUBGRAPH_ID=7XgdLW3bts4HktCYsu9dy8bEnuiNeZuftcuK3Aj4JXYV
UNISWAP_V4_BNB_SUBGRAPH_ID=EAq1nJKgjnuKH6Gj4RFjCW7LcL7E2uipbncdwV7TTWkX
UNISWAP_V3_AVALANCHE_SUBGRAPH_ID=6yLTrSzuv11uQizC1rZskEYbBve3Me5azjbY28MTAmhh
UNISWAP_V4_AVALANCHE_SUBGRAPH_ID=AbFwkbD1Gnj9vXwoLZMr5fo2xEs8MbSe9cf6TofvgYmK
UNISWAP_V3_CELO_SUBGRAPH_ID=6thLVqcdLLqhRKy1BzRn34VkfaQ1xfgt1m6eyPNM4e26

# Where the smart-money page lists positions on Base, OP Mainnet and Arbitrum,
# public like the rest (measured 2026-09-30; Arbitrum 2026-10-03). The v3 subgraphs the pool pages read
# there keep no positions, or refuse the query on a busy pool with "bad
# indexers"; these two answer it — Base's the largest pool's thousand positions
# in four seconds, OP Mainnet's in twenty-four, which the reader allows for.
# Arbitrum's keeps range edges as numbers rather than Tick entities, so it is
# asked a differently written query (chainEnvironment.ts says which); the
# largest pool's thousand positions came back in about twelve seconds.
# Mainnet's and Polygon's are the v3 ids above, which keep positions themselves.
UNISWAP_V3_BASE_POSITIONS_SUBGRAPH_ID=GqzP4Xaehti8KSfQmv3ZctFSjnSUYZ4En5NRsiTbvZpz
UNISWAP_V3_OPTIMISM_POSITIONS_SUBGRAPH_ID=Cghf4LfVqPiFw6fp6Y5X5Ubc8UpmUhSfJL82zwiBFLaj
UNISWAP_V3_ARBITRUM_POSITIONS_SUBGRAPH_ID=HyW7A86UEdYVt5b9Lrw8W2F98yKecerHKutZTRbSCX27

# Ethereum mainnet JSON-RPC endpoint, used server-side for read-only reads:
# eth_call, eth_getLogs and eth_getCode. Needed for the pool facts no
# subgraph exposes or gets right: a v3 pool's tickSpacing, and a v4 pool's fee
# — read from the key in the pool's own Initialize log and from its stored
# state, because the v4 subgraph's feeTier is the total a swap last paid, not
# the pool's fee. A v4 pool page cannot be shown without it.
#
# A free-tier endpoint is enough, and the reads are shaped for one: measured
# on 2026-09-16, such an endpoint answers about 150 requests in a burst and
# then refuses for a while, allows eth_getLogs a 10-block range at most, and
# charges a log query about three times a storage read. So logs are asked for
# one pool at a time at its creation block, in batches of ten, and only for
# hooked pools; every other fee comes from the storage word already read.
#
# Treat the whole URL as a secret. Most providers embed the API key in the path
# (https://.../v2/<KEY>), so this value is a credential even though it looks like
# a plain address — never prefix it with NEXT_PUBLIC_, and never log it.
# Read-only: this application never signs or sends anything.
ETHEREUM_RPC_URL=

# The same, for Base, Arbitrum One, Unichain, OP Mainnet, Polygon, BNB Chain,
# Avalanche and Celo. With an Alchemy endpoint above
# they are the same key under another host, and deploy/set-chain-rpcs.sh writes
# them from it on the server — tested by chain id, never typed or shown. Each
# network must be enabled on the provider's app. Same rules: a credential,
# server-side only, read-only.
BASE_RPC_URL=
ARBITRUM_RPC_URL=
UNICHAIN_RPC_URL=
OPTIMISM_RPC_URL=
POLYGON_RPC_URL=
BNB_RPC_URL=
AVALANCHE_RPC_URL=
CELO_RPC_URL=

# Where the Telegram links are kept: an Ethereum address beside the numeric id
# of the chat that asked to follow it, and which of that address's positions
# were inside their range last time. It is the only thing this application
# stores about a reader. The same Redis also keeps the smart-money page's
# measurements (liquiditywise:smart:*): pools, and the addresses of position
# holders, which anybody can read off the chain, for up to forty days — and
# nothing about who reads a page.
#
# A Redis on this machine. `redis://[:password@]host[:port][/database]`; the
# database index keeps these keys apart on a Redis something else also uses,
# and every key is prefixed `liquiditywise:` besides. Plain TCP, so this
# should be loopback or a private network — deploy/setup.sh installs a Redis
# bound to 127.0.0.1 and writes this line for you.
#
# With no store configured there are no Telegram alerts, and the page says so.
# Nothing else changes: the rate limit is counted in this process's own memory,
# which on one server is the whole truth rather than an approximation.
REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379/1

# Only for a deployment with nowhere to run a Redis of its own — a platform of
# short-lived processes, which is where this application used to live. Any
# endpoint that speaks the Upstash REST API will do. Ignored when REDIS_URL is
# set, and treat the URL as a credential alongside the token: together they are
# write access to the database.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# Optional. Telegram alerts: a reader links a chat to an address on the
# holdings page, and a scheduled pass tells them when one of that address's
# positions leaves its range or comes back. All four are needed together, and
# the Upstash pair above as well — that database is where the links are kept.
#
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN is what @BotFather hands out for the bot; it is the bot.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME is the public handle without the @, for the t.me link.
# TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET is any long random string; it is given to Telegram
#   at setWebhook time and Telegram presents it on every delivery, which is how
#   the webhook tells Telegram from anybody who found the URL.
# CRON_SECRET is what the scheduler presents as `Authorization: Bearer …` to
#   /api/telegram/check. On the server, deploy/setup.sh installs a cron entry
#   that reads it from this file and calls the route every five minutes.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME=
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
CRON_SECRET=

# Optional. Where this server reports its own faults: the numeric id of the
# chat the operator — you — has with the bot above. Send the bot any message
# and read the id out of `getUpdates`, or run
# `liquiditywise-health --hello` once it is installed to confirm it is right.
#
# With it set, deploy/health-check.sh writes to that chat when the site stops
# answering, when Redis stops answering, when the scheduled alert pass stops
# running, when the certificate is close to expiring, or when the disk is
# nearly full — once each, on the change, and once again when it clears.
#
# Not a credential on its own, but it is a chat id: keep it here with the rest
# rather than in the crontab.
TELEGRAM_OPERATOR_CHAT_ID=

# Optional, and recommended. The token of Server Watch, a second bot that only
# ever reports on the server, so the product bot above only ever talks to
# readers. Set it with deploy/set-server-watch-token.sh, which checks it is not
# the product bot's token and that the chat above can receive from it. Unset,
# the health check falls back to the product bot and says so in every message.
SERVER_WATCH_BOT_TOKEN=

# Optional, and needed once anybody links a chat. The daily backup of the
# Telegram links (deploy/backup.sh): encrypted to deploy/backup-recipient.pem,
# whose private key is not on the server, and sent to the Worker in
# deploy/backup-worker/, which keeps each copy seven days. Both are written by
# deploy/set-backup-secret.sh; the secret is random, made there, shared with
# that Worker and nothing else, and never typed or shown.
BACKUP_SECRET=
BACKUP_URL=
